0460: the trackpad's gestures as a Mac trackpad has them (boot-arg sweetpad=1; off by default), and two fixes.

The user wants macOS on the phone to feel made for touch. Until now two fingers scrolled in whole lines
(scrollEvent, 24 panel pixels a line): AppKit jumps three lines at a time, with no inertia.

With sweetpad=1 (SweetHIDTouch::padTouch / padTick):
- Two fingers: scroll events in points with pixel units, 1:1 with the fingers, with the gesture phases
  (Began / Changed / Ended in the event options) and, when the fingers leave while moving, momentum events from
  the 16 ms timer (Start, Continue with the speed falling 5 % a tick, End). A touch stops the momentum.
  The gesture starts after 40 px of two-finger movement, so a two-finger tap is still a right click.
- Three fingers: one swipe a gesture (150 px): up = control-up (Mission Control), down = control-down (the app's
  windows), left / right = control-right / -left (next / previous Space or full-screen app), sent through the
  on-screen keyboard's HID service. Three fingers never scroll or click.
- The finger left over after a scroll or swipe does not move the pointer.
- sweetuiscale=N: canvas pixels a point (default 2 for a canvas 1200 or more wide).
Always:
- When the primary finger lifts and another stays, the new primary's first sample is its start: the pointer no
  longer jumps by the distance between the two fingers (the lifted finger's position was taken as its last).
- The keyboard's summon swipe gives up on "downwards" only after 120 px, not 40 (S5y: a landing finger settled
  42 px lower within 24 ms and the swipe was handed to the trackpad).

Host test: tools/macos/padtest/run.sh (the driver's own pad and keyboard-touch code against touch sequences, both
modes). Not compiled for the kernel yet (the build tree was away): tools/macos/s5z-finish.sh compiles it into seq2f.

